WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.540
Marc Scott Zicree, Screenwriter/Author: I
am here to provide a service and if I can

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:08.910
open a door for someone where they understand
that it's not as hard as people make it sound,

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:10.470
you know that's the trick of it…

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:16.930
Film Courage: Marc, what do you think makes
you different than the thousands of other

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:20.491
screenwriters who came to Los Angeles during
your time here?

00:00:20.491 --> 00:00:22.550
What do you think made it different for you?

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:29.050
Marc: Well I think I was very, very lucky
in the fact that I was enthusiastic about

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:33.890
science fiction from when I was a small boy
and science fiction (unlike a lot of other

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:40.579
genres) the fans became the writers and so
they had a sense of wanting to pay it forward.

00:00:40.579 --> 00:00:44.050
When I noticed that the same writers were
writing for Star Trek and Outer Limits and

00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:48.030
Twilight Zone as we're writing the short stories
and books that I was reading and the novels

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:53.649
Ray Bradbury, Harlan Ellis and Theodore Sturgeon,
Richard Matheson, etc.

00:00:53.649 --> 00:00:58.239
I could then go to science fiction conventions
when I was a teenager and I sought these wonderful

00:00:58.239 --> 00:01:04.479
people out and they became mentors and friends
that that helped enormously and and also I

00:01:04.479 --> 00:01:09.509
just had such a passion for writing I had
such a I had a sense of calling from a very

00:01:09.509 --> 00:01:16.310
early age and I understood that because of
rod sterling because of so many of these writers

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:22.649
that I admired that you could aim high you
didn't have to blend in you just had to be

00:01:22.649 --> 00:01:29.770
clear what you were sharing with the world
and Ray Bradbury was a mentor of mine for

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:33.689
over 10 years i'd go over to his house once
a month for over 10 years toward the end of

00:01:33.689 --> 00:01:38.759
his life and ray always used to say look inward
don't look out for your mature look inward

00:01:38.759 --> 00:01:42.270
and that doesn't mean that you can't write
about things that are different from your

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:48.249
life it just means that you find your passions
and the your true self inward and then you

00:01:48.249 --> 00:01:55.429
put that out to the world and and so I had
extremely good teachers in that way because

00:01:55.429 --> 00:01:59.689
I think some writers out of fear feel that
they have to imitate others or write down

00:01:59.689 --> 00:02:04.789
to their audience I've never felt that I'm
the same as my audience and if I come across

00:02:04.789 --> 00:02:11.599
an idea or a story or characters or whatever
that excites me usually my audience will be

00:02:11.599 --> 00:02:18.409
excited too by what I'm doing and I trust
my audience I don't I they're they're I feel

00:02:18.409 --> 00:02:25.810
I'm with my family when when I'm talking to
all the weirdos weirdos are good things yeah

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:32.220
I like weirdos yeah yeah yeah how did you
know that you could actually get something

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:35.879
made I mean there's a lot of people who love
a certain genre but they dabble in it or they're

00:02:35.879 --> 00:02:40.420
on the sort of the outside the outer limits
so to speak how did you know though that you

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:45.409
could get something made and eventually be
successful at it well I didn't I didn't have

00:02:45.409 --> 00:02:50.400
a in terms of knowing that I would be successful
I didn't know that it was there are basically

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:54.640
two people who are responsible for my career
more than anyone which is my wife Elaine who

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:58.940
I met when I was 20. we got married when I
was 21 now we've been together 45 years and

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:03.680
married 44 and we write and produce and direct
together and my friend Michael Reeves who

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:08.920
was writing for television and brought me
into animation when I was like 22 23 years

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:15.609
old so I started by knowing by the time I
got out of art school about my degrees in

00:03:15.609 --> 00:03:19.540
painting sculpture and graphic arts from ucla
and because I always knew I was going to be

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:23.700
an artist or a writer but by the time I went
through college and then went to the clarion

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:29.831
writers workshop and sold my first short story
I knew I was going to be a writer and so I

00:03:29.831 --> 00:03:33.769
thought well how do you learn how to be a
writer in television and there was really

00:03:33.769 --> 00:03:38.379
no there was no graduate school in that at
the time we're talking the late 70s and so

00:03:38.379 --> 00:03:42.379
I thought well i'll write a book about one
of the greatest television shows ever made

00:03:42.379 --> 00:03:47.260
to learn how to make great television and
so that's why what gave me the idea to to

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:52.040
do the Twilight Zone Companion it was two
years after Rod Serling's death and had Rod

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:55.760
not died prematurely terribly sadly at age
50.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:59.000
I wouldn't have embarked upon that book because
if anyone should have written a book about

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:03.980
the Twilight Zone it would have been Rod but
since he wasn't with us anymore I thought

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:10.730
well let me follow this and see where it leads
now Elaine was invaluable because I started

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:14.810
writing the book interviewing over 100 people
who had worked on the show and it started

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:18.430
getting rejected when I started submitting
it it was rejected by 25 publishers over a

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:23.741
two-year period and probably if I would if
i'd been left to my own devices I would have

00:04:23.741 --> 00:04:28.020
probably quit and Elaine said no no just keep
going and but we also looked at what wasn't

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:35.250
working which was the agent I had wasn't the
right agent she wasn't she wasn't seeing opportunity

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:40.780
to close the deal and so we I switched agents
to someone who was young and hungry and she

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:44.840
sold it almost immediately to one of the publishers
who already rejected it but a different editor

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:48.509
who had grown up with the twilight zone and
understood why people would want to read about

00:04:48.509 --> 00:04:53.880
that and and the other thing I think that
was responsible for my success was sometimes

00:04:53.880 --> 00:05:00.800
people are so dogged about this is all i'll
do like I mean you know Elaine and I have

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:05.410
mentored a lot of people and one person they
said well I'm I want to act in a Steven Spielberg

00:05:05.410 --> 00:05:09.150
movie and we said well you know there's a
lot of other things being cast you could you

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:12.030
could try for other things no no Stephen's
it's got to be Steven Spielberg and so and

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:16.039
we said you know well he might only do one
movie every few years and and it might not

00:05:16.039 --> 00:05:20.790
have a role that's right for you you're limiting
your possibilities so I knew I wanted to be

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:25.310
in television and I knew that I wanted to
be writing for shows like star trek but when

00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:29.639
my friend Michael Reeves was writing animation
and he said would you like to come in and

00:05:29.639 --> 00:05:34.919
collaborate with me I saw that as that got
me into television and then I could write

00:05:34.919 --> 00:05:39.889
spec live action features and so forth and
that moved me then into live-action tv shows

00:05:39.889 --> 00:05:44.250
but that's how I ended up writing for Smurfs
and He-Man and super friends and and all those

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:49.460
shows and but I always brought my a-game to
everything I did so I wasn't writing down

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:54.750
at all someone once asked me when I was writing
for animation they said do you write for the

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:58.870
child in the audience and I said no I write
for the child and myself and so I would go

00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:03.780
to that place that would be delighted by the
jokes or by the stories and then just write

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:09.720
as truthfully as I possibly could and and
that worked and so I've I'm very proud of

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:14.590
the work I did in animation as well as the
work I've done in live action I am here to

00:06:14.590 --> 00:06:19.610
provide a service and if I can open a door
for someone where they understand that it's

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:25.629
not as hard as people make it sound you know
that's the trick of it you know it's like

00:06:25.629 --> 00:06:30.690
that's why I wrote green lighting yourself
so people would know that they could be the

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:36.090
engine that drives their career they don't
have to give up power they don't have to wait

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:40.020
for the studios and the networks or the publishing
companies even to see their value because

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:43.800
thanks to the internet thanks the fact we
all have cameras in our pockets thanks the

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:50.720
fact we can edit on on a mac or a pc there's
no reason not to just make the thing you want

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:56.289
to make give it all of your heart and soul
and put it out to the world that's your audience

00:06:56.289 --> 00:07:01.271
and that's how you build a body of work you
don't have to wait there's no reason to can

00:07:01.271 --> 00:07:06.130
you succeed in Hollywood if you're not a workaholic
yes you can succeed in Hollywood if you're

00:07:06.130 --> 00:07:11.850
not a workaholic but you do have to have a
work ethic and it's interesting in green lighting

00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:17.789
yourself in the new book I talk about having
a life and I deliberately put it after the

00:07:17.789 --> 00:07:23.630
chapter on building from success and recovering
from failure and I pointed I pointed out in

00:07:23.630 --> 00:07:27.292
the book that whether you're succeeding or
whether you're failing it's important to have

00:07:27.292 --> 00:07:33.300
a life separate from that because the separate
having people who love you family friends

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:39.461
travel doing things separate from the work
it gives you a breadth of experience but it

00:07:39.461 --> 00:07:46.520
also gives you a life it gives you something
that grounds you because you're not just someone

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:53.449
running after the brass ring and so actually
I know writers who have a very balanced life

00:07:53.449 --> 00:07:58.810
between family and the work and I think that's
a good idea I don't think one should work

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:04.159
all the time because you run out of things
to draw upon creatively but you also run out

00:08:04.159 --> 00:08:10.689
of I mean you just go crazy the isolation
Hollywood can drive people crazy simply because

00:08:10.689 --> 00:08:17.919
they get so materialistic they get so success-oriented
driven that they forget why they got involved

00:08:17.919 --> 00:08:22.219
in the first place you know I've known people
who've wanted to become who want to become

00:08:22.219 --> 00:08:26.629
writers because they want to get rich and
I've never seen it work maybe someone has

00:08:26.629 --> 00:08:32.200
succeeded that but the people that I've seen
succeed are the ones who just love the work

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:39.350
and they love what they're creating and they
are just so grateful for the opportunity and

00:08:39.350 --> 00:08:42.990
you know it's just there's something like
for instance I just came from a studio that

00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:51.140
I have that's filled with spaceship sets and
and you know we we're building an alien spaceship

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:56.780
we're building this hospital in a state a
space station and to be just and we're building

00:08:56.780 --> 00:09:01.590
eight-foot-tall creatures and just to be in
that world and be able to share that world

00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:08.070
with with everyone is is great but Elaine
[Zicree] and I try and stop working in the

00:09:08.070 --> 00:09:12.270
early evening we work at least five days a
week and sometimes we'll work weekends but

00:09:12.270 --> 00:09:17.560
we try not to but we we'll work and then the
evening for an hour or two we'll just relax

00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:22.350
and watch a movie or a tv show or something
or go for a walk or you know we and we have

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:28.020
own dogs and dogs force you to like get off
the sofa stop typing you know go take them

00:09:28.020 --> 00:09:34.390
for a walk and and that helps let the air
in a bit you can't I don't think you can be

00:09:34.390 --> 00:09:38.330
successful if you don't have a work ethic
and also I think you have to stand by your

00:09:38.330 --> 00:09:42.840
word the more you stand by your your word
the more you have a work ethic the more you

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:47.980
have at least craft if not I don't use the
word talent much because I've seen a lot of

00:09:47.980 --> 00:09:55.230
people who had talent who got nowhere whereas
if you have passion and craft and a work ethic

00:09:55.230 --> 00:10:04.101
and and you I mean you know the reason people
support me in so many ways is because if I

00:10:04.101 --> 00:10:07.530
say I'm going to do something I do it and
it doesn't matter if it's easier if it's hard

00:10:07.530 --> 00:10:12.140
if it's hard then it's hard it's I'm not going
to stop I'm not going to turn back I have

00:10:12.140 --> 00:10:17.960
I have work to do you know I get on with it
and my mantra to myself when things are difficult

00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:23.010
is you know I'm made of iron and nothing will
stop me and there are times for instance when

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:28.690
I did the star trek episode with George Takei
it was a very grueling shoot and and I said

00:10:28.690 --> 00:10:32.640
I'm made of iron and nothing will stop me
and I had to use that but we got to the finish

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:37.230
line and that got nominated for the Hugo and
the Nebula and I’m very very proud of that

00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:42.710
episode still and that was for Christina Moses's
first tv role we cast her out of the blue

00:10:42.710 --> 00:10:48.720
and she's now starring on a million little
things on abc so we saw what her amazing talent

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:54.380
was right from the beginning well you talked
about talent and talent is very long in this

00:10:54.380 --> 00:11:00.390
in this town but but what if someone's sort
of the pendulum swings more toward a hedonistic

00:11:00.390 --> 00:11:06.100
personality type well I try not to hang out
with meanies they're gonna be a really nice

00:11:06.100 --> 00:11:10.450
hedonist yeah they know but I mean I think

00:11:10.450 --> 00:11:17.140
you have to treat your body like a car that
you might own and you want to take care of

00:11:17.140 --> 00:11:21.550
it you want to make sure that you aren't burning
the candle at both ends you know so many people

00:11:21.550 --> 00:11:28.890
burn out and you know drugs alcohol you know
self-destructive behaviors gambling all that

00:11:28.890 --> 00:11:35.790
I it's just not sustainable in the long run
because it's just too hard on on your mind

00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:43.050
and your body you know it's you know I gravitate
toward people who have a family life most

00:11:43.050 --> 00:11:47.620
of the people I work with are married they
have many of them have kids I want someone

00:11:47.620 --> 00:11:52.360
who's stable I don't want someone where I
don't know if they're going to show up and

00:11:52.360 --> 00:11:57.710
so I can make the choice of who I work with
and I've been very lucky in terms of the actors

00:11:57.710 --> 00:12:03.270
I've worked with even when I was at the studios
and the networks I never had a crazy person

00:12:03.270 --> 00:12:08.700
I always had people who were there to do the
job and they appreciated the fact that I was

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:12.890
really putting putting everything I could
on the page and they would get these scripts

00:12:12.890 --> 00:12:17.770
they would love what I was writing because
it gave them something to act some emotional

00:12:17.770 --> 00:12:24.850
through line and and you know so quality work
gets you quality actors get you quality composers

00:12:24.850 --> 00:12:29.140
and get there's a ripple effect if you're
if you're doing your your best work but you

00:12:29.140 --> 00:12:34.251
can't be thinking about oh I've got this party
to go to I've got this no it's I mean that

00:12:34.251 --> 00:12:38.500
doesn't mean you can't have fun you can't
go on vacation you can't go water skiing or

00:12:38.500 --> 00:12:44.340
whatever you know and but but at the end of
the day you have to be reliable and that means

00:12:44.340 --> 00:12:50.660
you have to like look ahead you cannot be
such an impulse monster that you know it's

00:12:50.660 --> 00:12:55.970
Monday and you don't know where you're supposed
to be you know so can't wander the streets

00:12:55.970 --> 00:13:02.210
but you know so so that's yeah but of course
in you know in the new book I talk about if

00:13:02.210 --> 00:13:06.230
you if you have thrown together with someone
like that here's how you can deal with it

00:13:06.230 --> 00:13:10.680
because I had a friend who was an actress
and she was cast on tv show huge hit show

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:16.480
and the the the star of the show was drinking
all day and being very very abusive to people

00:13:16.480 --> 00:13:22.780
and very insulting and she just but she recognized
to not engage to do her work she was there

00:13:22.780 --> 00:13:27.270
to do her work and she did it and ironically
at the end of the day the actor came up and

00:13:27.270 --> 00:13:33.690
said you were great and so she had succeeded
I think often what we have to do is have our

00:13:33.690 --> 00:13:38.340
eye on the ball in terms of what we are there
to accomplish it's not about winning every

00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:44.660
single battle it's not about going toe-to-toe
with everyone it's not an alpha dog contest

00:13:44.660 --> 00:13:51.810
it's about what am I trying to accomplish
and so if you say okay well I'm here to write

00:13:51.810 --> 00:13:55.580
a great script and turn it into a great tv
show or I'm here to give a great performance

00:13:55.580 --> 00:14:00.200
whatever it is you have to be clear on it
but obviously the more that you take power

00:14:00.200 --> 00:14:05.030
the less you're going to be frustrated by
other people who are not there for the same

00:14:05.030 --> 00:14:09.070
purpose so that's why I have my own studio
now that's why I raise money the way I do

00:14:09.070 --> 00:14:15.630
because you know it's fun to be in charge
because then then there's no one around to

00:14:15.630 --> 00:14:19.470
I mean if anyone's gonna wreck it it'll be
me but then that's my fault you know it's

00:14:19.470 --> 00:14:25.270
like a failing of whatever but but it's so
it's so cool to work with great people and

00:14:25.270 --> 00:14:29.150
create work that that has meaning you know
I just couldn't be happier.

00:14:29.150 --> 00:14:30.150
Question For The Viewers:

00:14:30.150 --> 00:14:30.160
Can you succeed in Hollywood if you’re not
a workaholic?

